#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151956 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a8bf202-99c5-5bfd-8df3-5bd27cd65640" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Polynomial Vector Space Given Its Standard Monomial Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "2bcc7bb9-1b5e-5858-811d-1e5c14f0bc29" :question/math-academy-id "e-8288" :question/problem "What is the dimension of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $6?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$ is \n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_6[t])=6+1=7.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_n[t])=n+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151957 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "e1ae146f-04ae-5662-9ea3-178a23b3c366" :question/math-academy-id "e-8289" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $9?$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^8$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}_8[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $8$\n\n- $\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $3 \\times 3$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^8)=8\\neq 9.\\quad {\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{8}[t])=8+1=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n}(\\mathbb{R}))=n^2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}))=3^2=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151958 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "6d2ba662-f58e-56ec-bf87-c1f26c12c1c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8290" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$\n\n- $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $50 \\times 100$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$ and $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^{100}) = 100\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})) = 50\\cdot 100 = 5\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, t^n \\big\\},\\quad n\\in \\mathbb N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{i=0}^n a_i t^i = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$ if and only if $a_i = 0$ for all $i=0,1,2,\\ldots, n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in \\mathbb N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151959 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "61fa85c6-5b27-5f81-a7ba-363eb68e8d3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8315" :question/problem "What is the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $5\\times 6$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of $\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R})$ contains $3\\cdot 2= 6$ elements, and is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/assets/f2ab6a78388c9be5a4b46d63916c8e299cb95746547189ed9fe2783938c007dc.png)\n\nHowever, we're asked to find the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ not $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R}).$\n\nIf we were to repeat the same process above for the vector space $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ we would have $30$ matrices in our basis, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{5\\times 6}(\\mathbb{R})) = 5\\cdot 6 =30.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})) = n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a8bf202-99c5-5bfd-8df3-5bd27cd65640" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Polynomial Vector Space Given Its Standard Monomial Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3f62f28-f6fc-59c4-9873-174aa41490fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376813} {:answer/id #uuid "92d17cd6-bf31-53d2-9b87-dd9a06ec174c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376814} {:answer/id #uuid "83be7335-a6a2-574e-b639-3ca521baec04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376815} {:answer/id #uuid "cb60fe8e-4d2f-5274-9e1a-8e045fc6ea11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376816} {:answer/id #uuid "1a8a28db-a279-5f35-9cb2-c776cef8d7ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92d17cd6-bf31-53d2-9b87-dd9a06ec174c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376814} :answer-field/id #uuid "99e02988-df9b-596a-96be-40501664f025" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac90b5bc-97a8-5910-8e4c-691ef1d83be3" :question/math-academy-id "q-106865" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${R}_{15}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $15?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{15}[t]$ is \n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{15}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{15}[t])=15+1=16.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a8bf202-99c5-5bfd-8df3-5bd27cd65640" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Polynomial Vector Space Given Its Standard Monomial Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "554c7810-899e-5740-9675-11d0589757b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376820} {:answer/id #uuid "a4db9ddb-2498-555b-9f81-e3a9bd897786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376821} {:answer/id #uuid "ce08a372-6cfe-5371-8537-dc678aa85dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376822} {:answer/id #uuid "9f459a95-783b-5615-8da9-