-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd4be0b6-356f-59d4-908d-b25cc31dbbb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376848} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f6f926-9ac8-569f-8028-b28c48b31612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376849} {:answer/id #uuid "e28f2e83-efcc-5064-9571-bff2252f9f65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376850} {:answer/id #uuid "c805987d-6ecf-5e1a-9165-432eefffb698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376851} {:answer/id #uuid "16302b7b-882a-5e6d-bdd0-cb86af4af86c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd4be0b6-356f-59d4-908d-b25cc31dbbb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376848} :answer-field/id #uuid "36ef2cfb-8b2a-538c-a1b1-9bccc0b0017b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90418efb-5f81-5124-80f6-ce7467b7ec0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106888" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{8}(R),$ the vector space of all $8\\times 8$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n}(R)$ contains a total of ${n}^{2}$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ contains ${2}^{2}=4$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{8}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{8}(R))={8}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1707217-4772-55af-b365-031c3824ff50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376855} {:answer/id #uuid "59ea6012-76e8-5f01-a986-d1338a7d5d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376856} {:answer/id #uuid "99169741-7253-5aea-9adf-047c433e9ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376857} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4c4f67-387f-53eb-84df-56c30d95bf00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376858} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd1d889-3694-593d-9c62-40c3c502580c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99169741-7253-5aea-9adf-047c433e9ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376857} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac0c21ea-7ffd-5624-9383-95f50789059a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2eeef31b-4342-57a8-911e-633d1902fc4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106897" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $10?$\n\n- ${R}_{10}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $10$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{5}(R)$, the vector space of all $5\\times 5$ matrices over $R$\n- ${R}^{10}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{10}[t])=10+1=11\\ne 10.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(\\text{R}))={n}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{5}(R))={5}^{2}=25\\ne 10.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{10})=10.\\,✓\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "431a3acb-ddbf-5414-82b1-d9179fae9f6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376862} {:answer/id #uuid "ef224d18-d2d8-50f0-8882-6d9de91b4dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376863} {:answer/id #uuid "c9168d96-4a75-5552-85e9-b479abc7fe39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376864} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc58ad8-ba34-5853-a9ea-ca8443ddb766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376865} {:answer/id #uuid "0ad599c0-862b-50a6-b9df-bfcf149636c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ad599c0-862b-50a6-b9df-bfcf149636c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376866} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eb23244-e225-52e7-8c8a-cc565155a106" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08bf3d03-6691-5a28-9f42-839a1c9ba54b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106900" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $3?$\n\n- ${R}^{2}$\n- ${R}_{2}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{3}(R),$ the vector space of all $3\\times 3$ matrices over $R$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{2})=2\\ne 3.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{2}[t])=2+1=3.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3}(R))={3}^{2}=9\\ne 3.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ec9ccc6-5a60-5c05-9945-06756853743d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376869} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0835a5-3b50-51d9-ae30-835c1b352299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376870} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7697a2-0178-5cb6-adaf-419c590e16c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376871} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9b72e8-d0ae-512a-ae29-36b72b8a24fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376872} {:answer/id #uuid "9d918f0e-0d9a-5d11-bc36-d7a1fb7e0203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ec9ccc6-5a60-5c05-9945-06756853743d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376869} :answer-field/id #uuid "414e744a-a832-5c87-a355-4f512d602f63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21202e56-8c82-5a32-83c8-550cc4655885" :question/math-academy-id "q-106901" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $20?$\n\n- ${R}_{20}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $20$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{4\\times 5}(R),$ the vector space of all $4\\times 5$ matrices over $R$\n- ${R}^{20}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{20}[t])=20+1=21\\ne 20.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{4\\times 5}(R))=4\\cdot 5=20.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{20})=20.\\,✓\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a002214a-87b7-5306-a09e-e542c73063d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376876} {:answer/id #uuid "91057ccf-b96f-549d-8973-06280574b0f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376877} {:answer/id #uuid "e50e90bc-0136-594f-8cf2-34d8ad575992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376878} {:answer/id #uuid "574c1ae6-71c3-5484-b24f-d5ce733dc732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376879} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e8e873-add7-5433-ac6a-f655eca008b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a002214a-87b7-5306-a09e-e542c73063d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376876} :answer-field/id #uuid "04c0f25e-9b8c-51e2-91cf-86742a4cd75b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a94211ad-c433-579f-8c24-254aede14da5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106916" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- ${ℓ}^{1},$ the vector space of infinite real sequences where the sum of the absolute values of the terms are finite\n- ${R}_{3000}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3\\,000$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R),$ the vector space of all $50\\times 60$ matrices over $R$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${ℓ}^{1}$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of ${ℓ}^{1}$ is linearly independent: \n\n$$\n{{e}_{1},\\,{e}_{2},\\,…,{e}_{n}},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nwhere, for each $j\\in {1,2,…,n},$\n\n$$\n{e}_{j}=(0,…,0,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{1,}_{{↑}_{{j}^{\\text{th}}\\text{ position}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0,…).\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}=0\n$$\n\nif and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${ℓ}^{1}$ of size $n,$ which imples that  ${ℓ}^{1}$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${R}_{3000}[t]$ and  ${\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{3000}[t])=3\\,000+1=3\\,001,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R))=50\\cdot 60=3\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a8bf202-99c5-5bfd-8df3-5bd27cd65640" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Polynomial Vector Space Given Its Standard Monomial Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83ddb001-c66c-5cde-82ba-ae0b5587788e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149917} {:answer/id #uuid "a3391a57-058e-5e75-84eb-2a59d955d27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149918} {:answer/id #uuid "d0639148-b63d-5d85-b52b-ab06459c137b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149919} {:answer/id #uuid "11d9138e-a3ad-5b09-b5d6-41f0293a7839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149920} {:answer/id #uuid "41d24a0e-73ca-5d4d-ab73-1b5fcf42907c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41d24a0e-73ca-5d4d-ab73-1b5fcf42907c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149921} :answer-field/id #uuid "79083a6d-fb71-5df1-b8a6-b6551be6ec1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "090e8cd2-3587-589a-a954-4b48d6d7a9fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-36471" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${R}_{7}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $7?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{7}[t]$ is \n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{7}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{7}[t])=7+1=8.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "004b4d60-9321-5864-bb3b-2ee96afc85f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376883} {:answer/id #uuid "7293dd5b-8ed2-5aef-8b53-61a0f3c4933f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376884} {:answer/id #uuid "e6653740-e9ee-5d4b-9c71-6a0473b7a716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376885} {:answer/id #uuid "c92a9a07-841e-5c4e-bdda-79fddb09b3da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376886} {:answer/id #uuid "fa85a51d-6ce1-56ab-9eeb-f9b906d61d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7293dd5b-8ed2-5aef-8b53-61a0f3c4933f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376884} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7d05a45-9f31-5753-8e5b-21f03b15a810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ffbd9a8-0125-537f-b30a-fb56bc0ed6f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95217" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{10\\times 2}(R),$ the vector space of all $10\\times 2$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{10\\times 2}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{10\\times 2}(R))=10\\cdot 2=20.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86aa432d-a140-5f18-8a3c-0fb493cd26f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376890} {:answer/id #uuid "d178093c-1985-5e3f-9a49-40051af5a1bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376891} {:answer/id #uuid "fc56f211-bac2-50e1-807c-87b4af5a2c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376892} {:answer/id #uuid "d764dec1-8491-5e6e-99d6-c5ba2eb24a10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376893} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb677a5-23a1-5142-a7ac-c9660f46e6b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d764dec1-8491-5e6e-99d6-c5ba2eb24a10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376893} :answer-field/id #uuid "edc52d4d-d2d7-53dc-9e0c-cdcf237d8f32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75fa5711-6064-5789-9b85-9d315ddc5ebd" :question/math-academy-id "q-95220" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{5}(R),$ the vector space of all $5\\times 5$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n}(R)$ contains a total of ${n}^{2}$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ contains ${2}^{2}=4$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{5}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{5}(R))={5}^{2}=25.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247b038c-e0b8-5748-9fcc-43734822ec29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376897} {:answer/id #uuid "8df1985f-3cde-5a82-9518-caae2dbd541a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376898} {:answer/id #uuid "d3adea68-3d38-52e0-a268-f18922bf0da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376899} {:answer/id #uuid "2f90239e-37c4-5495-87ce-fdde7bb59493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376900} {:answer/id #uuid "c9777171-128d-59e7-bd50-43cc24807ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3adea68-3d38-52e0-a268-f18922bf0da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376899} :answer-field/id #uuid "b72d0ee4-6a17-581b-b04d-9605eca697b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8aab625a-c16b-563d-aada-04a36e4029ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-95221" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{4}(R),$ the vector space of all $4\\times 4$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n}(R)$ contains a total of ${n}^{2}$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ contains ${2}^{2}=4$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{4}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{4}(R))={4}^{2}=16.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bc8e09b-ec5c-520f-b979-385547908338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376904} {:answer/id #uuid "0799db97-eb2a-5652-a4b5-58ed590f0a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376905} {:answer/id #uuid "17c003f6-7ccb-5758-81e4-fb695d143e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376906} {:answer/id #uuid "9f92a436-aa50-5822-a0b1-875b1ab2e2ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376907} {:answer/id #uuid "2f68bd85-aad1-54fc-b806-3c6a7f5142b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0799db97-eb2a-5652-a4b5-58ed590f0a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376905} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e028ff5-436b-5228-bc6e-693fc2804cec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbfeb240-647c-5d47-9747-c8dbe7eae1a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95226" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $15?$\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 5}(R),$ the vector space of all $3\\times 5$ matrices over $R$\n- ${R}_{15}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $15$\n- ${R}^{15}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3\\times 5}(R))=3\\cdot 5=15.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{15}[t])=15+1=16\\ne 15.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{15})=15.\\,✓\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e7f0eb5-9ebb-56ca-a633-21a5d4ffc276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376911} {:answer/id #uuid "0aef4eec-3f78-569f-bef9-e62321696a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376912} {:answer/id #uuid "11a742b6-0150-55e3-a78c-c96105e26f84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376913} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7856a5-af78-582b-8ce7-d67160b1998a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376914} {:answer/id #uuid "cca5d9c4-3442-56a4-ad2c-d72e0d98097e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e7f0eb5-9ebb-56ca-a633-21a5d4ffc276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376911} :answer-field/id #uuid "095825d6-33b5-5040-921a-ef4ba4ac56a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26cb6bca-0a5a-5f2c-ba36-021c5506b021" :question/math-academy-id "q-95230" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $36?$\n\n- ${R}_{6}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $6$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{12\\times 3}(R),$ the vector space of all $12\\times 3$ matrices over $R$\n- ${R}^{36}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{6}[t])=6+1=7\\ne 36.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{12\\times 3}(R))=12\\cdot 3=36.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{36})=36.\\,✓\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cf907f4-6b6f-560c-a19e-861b019de6e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376918} {:answer/id #uuid "0c5a3a2e-139b-5282-86be-8c03b481d403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376919} {:answer/id #uuid "899e2062-6bb8-5049-8b5d-2dcd13d458b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376920} {:answer/id #uuid "57dee912-3916-566b-ae8c-cb4eb0cfd114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376921} {:answer/id #uuid "81dcddfb-95aa-5f91-b89c-82c291d00108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "899e2062-6bb8-5049-8b5d-2dcd13d458b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376920} :answer-field/id #uuid "2776a324-97de-5aa8-be3e-813aa6d3b28a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52012800-e718-588f-a79c-9254035c24b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-95239" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- ${R}^{200}$\n- $R[t],$ the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{200}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 200$ matrices over $R$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $R[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{a}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${a}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $R[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $R[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{200}(R)$ and ${R}^{200}$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{200}(R))={200}^{2}=40\\,000,\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{200})=200\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ea024fd-cfc4-524a-8e01-5c85ee9f9576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376925} {:answer/id #uuid "969af205-2c6e-5119-a92f-7781a4cb884b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376926} {:answer/id #uuid "8556b682-a2e3-5c8a-b53c-0f18138bffb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376927} {:answer/id #uuid "b51adb22-7803-5f46-ad8a-67d12578eb52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376928} {:answer/id #uuid "294ead6c-51c5-5a9b-932f-67c2639c1917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ea024fd-cfc4-524a-8e01-5c85ee9f9576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376925} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a4ee5dc-d1aa-52fa-803f-e870bd84d783" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03206f5b-8fe5-5de2-9b76-68d8a9d6bf34" :question/math-academy-id "q-95240" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- ${R}_{1000}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $1\\,000$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 500}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 500$ matrices over $R$\n- $C,$ as a vector space over $R$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${R}_{1000}[t]$ and ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 500}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{1000}[t])=1\\,000+1=1\\,001,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{200\\times 500}(R))=200\\cdot 500=100\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\n- $C$ is also finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of complex numbers is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,i}\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\na\\cdot 1+b\\cdot i=0\n$$\n\nif and only if $a=0$ and $b=0.$ Since our vector space is also spanned by this linearly independent subset, we have that $\\text{dim}(C)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4d10777-90b5-50c6-9489-2e40539a1581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376932} {:answer/id #uuid "cbfccd02-7c00-5e95-8003-84a36c86153d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376933} {:answer/id #uuid "503f44d5-ea1d-5db1-8bcb-203193ab85a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376934} {:answer/id #uuid "96328657-2f4f-5e4f-adb0-431520404f47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376935} {:answer/id #uuid "37b23975-5716-50bd-bc36-962e44b0fae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbfccd02-7c00-5e95-8003-84a36c86153d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376933} :answer-field/id #uuid "281c86c4-24bb-5bcb-a449-30fad623275d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c38c945-de56-52ec-838b-326477ef9cc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-95251" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{250\\times 100}(R),$ the vector space of all $250\\times 100$ matrices over $R$\n- $R[t],$ the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$\n- ${R}_{10}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $10$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $R[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{a}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${a}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $R[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $R[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{250\\times 100}(R)$ and ${R}_{10}[t]$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{250\\times 100}(R))=250\\cdot 100=25\\,000\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{10}[t])=10+1=11,\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5afaa353-f503-5fed-9b2a-6c2fbae734aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376939} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb94719-1bac-55cd-86f1-1bc68b3ca677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376940} {:answer/id #uuid "bd566fd0-5377-58bd-8898-89c3a6274984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376941} {:answer/id #uuid "5014a780-e925-547c-8700-afa6f0bea04c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376942} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f1b72a-dbe6-5c1b-a123-7865fb4fa532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3f1b72a-dbe6-5c1b-a123-7865fb4fa532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376943} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ce4ff74-f807-5564-8760-1d650be00d74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06369acb-8474-5f1b-8907-84f14457a087" :question/math-academy-id "q-95386" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- ${R}^{150}$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{150}(R),$ the vector space of all $150\\times 150$ matrices over $R$\n- $R[t],$ the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${R}^{150}$ and ${\\text{M}}_{150}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{150})=150\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{150}(R))={150}^{2}=22\\,500,\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\n- $R[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{a}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${a}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $R[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $R[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
